  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The coplanarity of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> results from a delicate balance of the repulsive forces, which favour the skew structure, and of the effects of bonding which favour the planar structure.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is a bent molecule.&nbsp;The<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo></math>molecule is a trigonal planar molecule.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> has a linear, unsymmetrical structure that may be thought of as a hybrid of two resonance forms.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0036_6f2b51556185.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 289px;\" /></p></blockquote>"
